  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM MDT Fri Jul 10 2026\n\nA weak shortwave trough is present over the northern Great \nBasin today. The models have been picking up on mid level \nmoisture around 500 mb associated with that trough. This is \nconfirmed by satellite imagery during the overnight hours \nshowing some patchy mid level clouds across the northern Great \nBasin. The combination of steep lapse rates with the incoming \nheat and the added weak lift from this trough, the models are \npicking up on some isolated dry thunderstorms across central and\neast Idaho this afternoon. Gusty outflow winds are possible \nthis afternoon given the steep lapse rates. Most of this \nactivity should dissipate by 03z this evening.\n\nStill seeing very hot temperatures for the weekend with the center \nof a strong ridge moving through the central Great Basin. Temps will \nbe approaching upper 90s to around 100 on Saturday with 100 degree \ntemps looking more widespread on Sunday. A combination of heat \nadvisories and warnings are in effect for the weekend. The warnings \nare mainly along the Utah border, closer to the more intense heat. \nBear Lake County is in the advisory with higher base elevations \nthere offering a couple degrees of very slightly cooler temps; \nenough to affect the heat risk values. Breezy conditions are likely \nacross the weekend as well with the stronger winds across the central \nmountains into the upper Snake River Plain. Warnings and advisories \nmay need to be extended into Monday. The temps on Monday look \nslightly cooler as the center of the ridge moves into the northern \nPlains, but temps will still be in the upper 90s to around 100 \nacross much of the area. As we head into mid week and beyond the \nheat backs off a little with highs expected to be in the low and mid \n90s. We get into a monsoonal pattern next week where the storms and \nclouds will help take the edge off the heat.",
